<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Lake George</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1755">1755</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Lake George was fought on 8 September 1755 at the southern end of Lake George in the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-and-indian-war">French and Indian War</a>. British colonial troops under William Johnson and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> warriors under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrick-theyanoguin">Hendrick Theyanoguin</a>, who was killed, repulsed an attack by French regulars, Canadians and allied Native warriors under Baron Dieskau, who was captured. The British then built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-william-henry">Fort William Henry</a> on the site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-george.jpg" alt="Battle of Lake George" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin West · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johnson_saving_Dieskau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1755 General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-braddock">Edward Braddock</a> and the provincial governors planned four campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-johnson">Sir William Johnson</a>, who had no experience of war, was given the task of taking Fort St. Frédéric at Crown Point. His army of colonial volunteers from Massachusetts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a>, Rhode Island and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> reached the southern end of the Lac du Saint Sacrement on 28 August 1755, and he renamed it Lake George in honour of George II.</p><p>Baron Dieskau, learning from a prisoner that only 500 men held Fort Lyman, later Fort Edward, set out on 2 September with a column of 1,500 to cut Johnson's supplies. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abenaki">Abenaki</a> allies would not attack a fort they expected to have <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, so on the morning of 8 September he turned towards Lake George instead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Johnson sent Colonel Ephraim Williams south with 1,000 provincial troops and 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrick-theyanoguin">Hendrick Theyanoguin</a>. Warned by a deserter, Dieskau laid an ambush in a ravine, which was set off early when Christian Iroquois on the French side saw relatives in the column and fired warning shots. The column collapsed in what became known as the Bloody Morning Scout: Williams was killed, Hendrick was bayoneted after his horse was shot, and the French lost Jacques Legardeur de Saint-Pierre, commander of their Canadians and Indians.</p><p>The survivors fled to Johnson's camp, where he threw up barricades of wagons, overturned boats and felled trees. Dieskau led his grenadiers along the lake road, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahnawake">Caughnawagas</a> would not attack a camp defended by hundreds of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> kinsmen, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abenaki">Abenakis</a> and Canadians held back with them. Johnson's three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, firing grapeshot, broke the advance, and Dieskau was shot while calling the Canadians forward. Around 4 pm the French withdrew, and that evening a detachment from Fort Edward under Nathaniel Folsom attacked them near Bloody Pond.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Dieskau, left on the field, was carried to Johnson's tent, and Johnson protected him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawks</a> who wished to burn him for Hendrick's death. Accounts of the losses conflict: Ian K. Steele gives 331 killed, wounded and missing on the British side and 339 on the French. Johnson received a baronetcy and £5,000, while Dieskau was censured and spent much of his life in captivity. Before the battle Williams had made a will at Albany for the foundation of Williams College.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Lake George” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century Mohawk warriors under Hendrick Theyanoguin fought beside the British at the <b>Battle of Lake George</b> in 1755, and in the American Revolution the league’s nations fought one another, notably at the Battle of Oriskany in 1777.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Military</a></li></ul></section>
